    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's the thing, there isn't really a \"reconnecting\" part left to find. the version of me that had a future, the one people actually cared about and recognized... that person is dead. they died the day the injury happened. i'm just a ghost haunting a body that doesn't even work anymore. it's like i'm just waiting for the rest of me to catch up to how finished i already feel.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is incredibly painful to feel like the \"real\" you died with the injury, leaving you as a ghost in a body that no longer feels like home. This profound sense of self-erasure can make the physical act of healing feel futile when the identity that *should* be healing is already gone.\n\nEven so, sometimes the \"you\" that is left behind is the very part that *desperately* needs to heal the \"you\" that was hurt.",
